<h2>eu-inc Weekly | 2026-W10</h2>
<p>No new signal arrived this week; coverage remained anchored to the same foundational explainers and startup advocacy that dominated January and February.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.euractiv.com/news/five-ways-to-an-eu-inc/">Five ways to an EU Inc</a> (Euractiv) -- maps the five legal-instrument options (regulation, directive, etc.) and explains why the choice of pathway could determine whether the reform succeeds or stalls in Council.</p>
<p>💡 <a href="https://www.europeanbusinessreview.com/eu-inc-may-solve-legal-issues-but-scaling-across-borders-still-depends-on-how-teams-work/">EU Inc. May Solve Legal Issues, But Scaling Across Borders Still Depends on How Teams Work</a> (European Business Review) -- argues that a unified incorporation structure removes a visible barrier but leaves cross-border HR, payroll, and culture friction entirely untouched.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.boersen-zeitung.de/unternehmen-branchen/startups-fordern-verordnung-zur-eu-inc">Startups fordern Verordnung zur EU Inc</a> (Börsen-Zeitung) -- German startup voices specifically demand a regulation (not a directive) and flag capital-gains exit taxes triggered by cross-border founder relocations as a critical unresolved problem.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.fieldfisher.com/en/insights/eu-inc-towards-a-single-european-company-framework">EU Inc.: towards a single European company framework?</a> (Fieldfisher) -- law-firm primer noting that Ireland is pushing back on including tax and labour harmonisation in the EU Inc. scope, consistent with earlier reporting from the Irish Business Post.</p>]]></description>
  </item>
